  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "If $x<2,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Here, the variable limit $x$ appears as the lower limit of integration. To apply FTC II directly, we prefer the variable on the upper limit. Using the property of definite integrals, we rewrite\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow, by FTC II we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
